# Idea Brief: Hosted Cross-Project Tool Shed Dashboard

Status: exploring
Type: idea-brief
Updated: 2026-08-29
Next Action: continue discovery around authentication, payload contract, qualification targets, and deployment gates, or promote with `ts: prm idea IDEA-ID`
Produces:

## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Add an organized web dashboard whose canonical landing page is
`https://ts.rookaro.com/dashboard/`. It receives sanitized status reports from multiple local Tool
Shed instances and lets the operator move quickly among projects. Each local instance remains
authoritative for its own work state; the hosted service is an advisory reporting and presentation
layer, not a remote controller or replacement work database.

The site should establish one reusable dashboard application shell: a persistent header and
minimal footer, a left navigation pane for fleet and project selection, and a right content pane
for the selected scope. Long project pages should be divided into one level of URL-addressable tabs
so summary and attention information stays visible without forcing the operator through a single
large document.

The current public site's visual presentation is a compatibility contract. Its header, footer,
typography, colors, spacing, responsive behavior, public routes, and documentation behavior must
remain intact. The implementation stack may change when the change produces a concrete functional,
reliability, performance, security, deployment, or recovery benefit. The selected hosted direction
keeps nginx as the single exposed gateway, continues serving the existing documentation as static
content, and adds one Django application plus PostgreSQL for the authenticated dashboard and
reporting API.

An installation does not receive reporting trust automatically. The proposed
`ts dashboard connect` action initiates a short-lived device-style pairing request, the dashboard
notifies an authenticated operator, and only explicit approval converts that request into one
revocable instance credential. The local client polls for the decision over outbound HTTPS, so the
server never needs an inbound path to a workstation.

Each Tool Shed instance should use one singleton background reporting worker. Local update triggers
durably enqueue a sanitized event and invoke a lightweight launcher. The launcher starts the worker
when absent; if the singleton lock is already held, it exits because the active worker polls the
outbox every second. While active, the worker drains the outbox, sends changes, and emits a heartbeat
every one minute. It declares itself quiescent and exits after two hours with no activity and an
empty outbox. A periodic safety check recovers missed triggers, direct database changes, crashes,
and unsent events.

App Server operability should be visible across the fleet rather than inferred from occasional
campaign evidence. Every reporting instance should maintain durable cumulative counts for App
Server execution attempts and App Server failures, plus the minimum status needed to interpret
them: enabled/configured state, qualification or availability state, last successful use, last
failure, client/App Server version, and fallback outcome. A failure means the App Server path itself
failed or was unusable; a campaign, test, or product failure after a healthy App Server execution
must not inflate the App Server failure count.

The dashboard should have a dedicated fleet-level App Server screen with per-project and
per-instance drilldown. It should show adoption and health state, usage and failure totals, failure
rate, recent failure time, fallback count, affected versions/platforms, and sanitized failure
groups. The web site remains informational: it does not invoke mitigation, send commands, or mutate
an instance. When the screen reveals a problem, the operator goes to the Tool Shed maintainer,
requests the selected server report through a maintainer-side read-only command, and performs any
diagnosis or mitigation through the maintainer's governed local workflow.

Tool Shed work efficiency should also be visible by project. Each reporting instance should send a
versioned aggregate produced by IDEA-0011's local workflow journal: exact
`remedial_tokens_actual` only for provider-measured phases, `remedial_token_coverage`, and separate
remedial interaction, output-byte, duration, and retry proxies for all supported provider paths.
The dashboard should provide a fleet-level Work Efficiency view with one row per project and
project/instance drilldown. It must place coverage, measurement window, freshness, and counter
epoch beside token totals, display missing usage as unknown rather than zero, and never rank or
compare projects as if partial token coverage were complete.

### Why It Matters

VS Code presents only the current workspace and does not give the operator a useful portfolio view
while several projects are progressing in parallel. The operator needs one surface that exposes
what needs attention, what is working, what recently completed, what has an open outcome loop, and
which instances are stale without opening each repository or navigating a large generated file
tree.

Immediate update triggers make the hosted view responsive, while a short-lived resident worker
provides heartbeats and retry behavior without requiring every production install to run a
permanent daemon. Singleton coordination prevents duplicate launch attempts from accumulating
background processes.

### Desired Outcome

The operator opens `https://ts.rookaro.com/dashboard/` and, after authentication, sees every
reporting Tool Shed project ordered by operational relevance. Selecting a project changes the
right pane without losing fleet navigation. Important state remains above the content tabs, and
each detailed section has a stable URL suitable for refresh, browser history, and direct linking.

The operator can also open a dedicated App Server fleet screen to see which instances actually use
App Server, how often they use it, how many App Server-path failures occurred, which failure groups
are recurring, and where GUI fallback is masking a reliability problem. The operator can select a
bounded cohort or failure group, then request that exact informational report from the maintainer
without copying raw database content or treating the hosted dashboard as a repair authority.

The operator can open a Work Efficiency screen to compare per-project remedial work. Projects with
measured provider tokens show exact remedial tokens and coverage; GUI-native or partially measured
projects show clearly separated tool-call, output, duration, and retry proxies. The screen supports
trend windows and project drilldown without uploading prompts, source, paths, commands, or raw
diagnostics, and it identifies stale, incompatible, or reset counter epochs instead of joining them
into misleading totals.

Existing visitors continue to receive the same visual public site at `/`, `/guide/`, `/help/`, and
`/ref/`. The dashboard reuses that design language without making the public documentation require
authentication or a dynamic application round trip.

Local changes become visible promptly. During an active work session, the server normally receives
a heartbeat every minute. A duplicate reporter launch observes the held singleton lock and exits;
the existing worker reads the new event on its next one-second outbox poll. Network outages, worker
crashes, direct SQLite access, and retries do not lose events or create duplicate server state. An
idle worker exits cleanly after two hours, and the server distinguishes intentional quiescence from
an overdue or incompatible client.

## First-Release Boundary

The first release is intentionally one deployable vertical slice:

- Preserve the existing public site's static presentation and nginx gateway.
- Add one Django application and one PostgreSQL instance, without replicas or high-availability
  orchestration.
- Support one authenticated maintainer. Reuse an existing OIDC provider when available; otherwise
  use a maintained Django-compatible login with MFA. Do not deploy a new identity provider solely
  for this dashboard.
- Support device-style instance enrollment, approval, rejection, revocation, and re-pairing. Store
  the resulting scoped token in Tool Shed's existing protected user-local credential state.
- Provide a project list ordered by attention plus Overview, Current Work, Outcome Reconciliation,
  and Health tabs.
- Provide one dedicated fleet-level App Server screen with per-instance health, cumulative use and
  failure counts, fallback counts, recent failure state, version/platform grouping, and bounded
  sanitized failure details suitable for later maintainer-side review.
- Provide one fleet-level Work Efficiency screen with per-project and per-instance drilldown for
  exact measured remedial tokens, measurement coverage, and separately labelled remedial
  interactions, output bytes, duration, and retries. Unknown or partial token coverage must remain
  visually explicit.
- Provide a maintainer-side read-only report request that retrieves a selected App Server cohort or
  failure group from the hosted service. Diagnosis, code changes, release, deployment, and instance
  mutation remain outside the web application.
- Retain current reported state, `last_seen`, quiescent state, and material state-change events. Do
  not retain or roll up one-minute heartbeat history.
- Use the durable local outbox, one singleton worker, an atomic lock, one-second outbox polling, a
  one-minute active heartbeat, a two-hour idle exit, and a periodic safety pass.
- Qualify representative public desktop and mobile views plus authentication, ingestion, health,
  backup, restore, migration, and rollback.

Defer until measured need or operator evidence justifies expansion:

- Pinned projects, custom groups or tags, advanced filters, detailed Roadmap & Gates views, and
  general Activity/History browsing.
- Remote mitigation, dashboard-triggered diagnostics, server-to-instance commands, and automatic
  failure repair.
- Server-sent events; use bounded browser polling in the first release.
- Heartbeat-history storage or rollups and operating-system-specific keyring integrations beyond
  the existing protected user-local credential mechanism.
- A new identity-provider service, mutual TLS, custom request signing, PostgreSQL replicas or HA,
  blue-green or multi-region deployment, Redis, Celery, Kubernetes, a message broker, a full SPA,
  and remote work control.

## PRM Handoff And Closed-Loop Governance

Promotion must open a governed outcome loop rooted in the immutable identity and accepted revision
of `IDEA-0007`; changing a title, path, map, roadmap, or campaign must not replace that origin. The
project map, Program Roadmap, milestones, and campaigns become related child loops whose results
propagate to the idea outcome.

The PRM handoff must carry:

- the accepted first-release outcome and every requirement derived from this brief;
- every later material change to outcome, scope, behavior, target, acceptance, authority, privacy,
  or safety, including authorization and the evidence that must be rerun;
- current product-truth references for source, build, released client, hosted application,
  PostgreSQL schema, and the deployed `ts.rookaro.com` target;
- endpoint-appropriate evidence for tests, visual compatibility, authentication, ingestion,
  upgrade, backup/restore, rollback, deployment, and a disconnected installed-client canary; and
- an explicit final verdict, reconciliation state, residual-work disposition, and upward
  propagation through every owning loop.

A completed campaign, terminal roadmap, successful HTTP request, deployed server, or empty queue is
not sufficient closure. `IDEA-0007` closes only after the accepted idea is reconciled against
authorized changes, current product truth, target evidence, and residual work.

PRM should freeze the reporting allowlist/privacy contract, human authentication choice,
first-release fleet and supported-platform qualification target, safety-check cadence, material
event retention, and backup/restore acceptance targets before their dependent implementation or
production gates. These are roadmap decisions and evidence gates, not blockers to promotion.

## Dashboard Template

### Header

- Tool Shed brand plus an explicit Dashboard marker.
- Global project search or project switcher.
- Reporting freshness or connection summary.
- Authenticated account and sign-out controls.
- Persistent or sticky positioning where it does not obscure content.

### Left Navigation Pane

- All projects, needs attention, working, and stale/offline.
- A dedicated App Server fleet destination with unhealthy and recurring-failure attention counts.
- Searchable project list with state counts. Pinned projects and custom groups or tags are deferred.
- Clear separation between portfolio navigation and the section tabs inside the right pane.
- Collapsible layout on desktop and a drawer on narrow screens.

### Right Content Pane

- Selected fleet or project title, current state, last report time, and any attention condition.
- Compact summary cards above the tabs so blocked, stale, or unreconciled state is never hidden.
- First-release project tabs: Overview, Current Work, Outcome Reconciliation, and Health. Detailed
  Roadmap & Gates and Activity/History views are deferred.
- First-release fleet tabs may specialize the same content area for Overview, Needs Attention,
  Working, and Stale views.
- The dedicated App Server screen should support fleet, project, instance, version/platform, and
  sanitized failure-group views without nesting another tab hierarchy.
- One tab level only. Long lists use paging or incremental loading; record details use a drawer or
  focused detail route rather than nested tabs.
- Sticky tab and table headers where useful. On narrow screens, tabs scroll horizontally or become
  an accessible compact selector.

### Footer

- Dashboard/server version, reporting API status, documentation, privacy/retention information,
  and support or source links.
- No primary operational controls in the footer.

### Navigation And Accessibility Contract

- Render tabs as stable routes such as `/dashboard/projects/{project-id}/outcomes`, with progressive
  enhancement rather than JavaScript-only hidden state.
- Preserve browser back/forward behavior, reload, direct links, focus state, and keyboard operation.
- Fetch or mount only the active tab's detailed data, while keeping shared summary and attention
  state visible.
- Do not use tabs for information that must be compared simultaneously.

## Hosted Service Architecture

### Source, GitHub, And Deployment Authority

- Keep first-release public-site and dashboard source in the existing
  `PC-Redemption/tool_shed` GitHub repository. Track canonical public pages and assets under `site/`,
  the Django application and migrations in a repository-owned source directory selected during
  PRM, versioned API and payload contracts, deployment templates, tests, and operator documentation.
- Keep `build/ts.rookaro.com/` generated and ignored. The deployed
  `/home/jon/docker/ts.rookaro.com/` directory on `sup` is also a generated release copy, not a Git
  working tree or a second source repository.
- Never commit the live PostgreSQL database, database storage, backups, reporter credentials,
  Django secrets, host-local configuration, logs, runtime status, or enrollment tokens. Track only
  safe examples, schemas, migrations, and operational contracts.
- Keep the Rookaro router configuration under its existing separate production ownership. This
  repository may document the required `ts.rookaro.com` route, but it does not take editable
  authority over `/home/jon/docker/rookaro.com` or production credentials.
- Split the dashboard into a separate GitHub repository only if measured independent release
  cadence, ownership, access-control, or deployment needs outweigh the coordination cost. A second
  repository is not part of the first-release design.

### Request Boundary

- Preserve the existing Rookaro hostname route and host port. The `ts.rookaro.com` Compose project
  continues to expose only one nginx gateway.
- Serve `/`, `/guide/`, `/help/`, `/ref/`, and shared public assets directly from nginx.
- Reverse proxy `/dashboard/` to the authenticated application and `/api/v1/` to the versioned
  instance-reporting API over the internal Compose network.
- Keep the dashboard and API same-origin with the public site to avoid unnecessary CORS and browser
  credential complexity.

### Application And Data

- Use one Django application for server-rendered dashboard pages, sessions, CSRF protection,
  migrations, data access, and the reporting API rather than assembling separate UI, auth, ORM, and
  API services.
- Use PostgreSQL as the hosted reporting store so concurrent instance ingestion, dashboard reads,
  schema migration, retention, and backup do not depend on one application process. Local Tool Shed
  SQLite databases remain unchanged and authoritative for their own project state. The first
  release operates one PostgreSQL instance and does not add replicas or HA orchestration.
- Store registered projects and instances, current reported state, last heartbeat, quiescent state,
  sanitized state-change events, sequence/idempotency records, client/protocol versions, and
  credential metadata. Store the latest App Server health snapshot and cumulative counter epoch,
  execution-attempt count, failure count, fallback count, last-success/last-failure timestamps, and
  bounded sanitized failure groups. Store the latest per-project Work Efficiency aggregate with its
  schema version, measurement window, counter epoch, freshness, token-coverage numerator and
  denominator, exact measured remedial-token totals, and separate proxy totals. Update `last_seen`
  and current presence in place, retain only material state-change, App Server failure, and bounded
  Work Efficiency window changes, and do not build heartbeat-history or granular model-activity
  storage in the first release.
- Use server-rendered HTML with small, dependency-light JavaScript enhancements. Server-sent events
  are deferred; bounded browser polling refreshes first-release views. A large client-side SPA is
  not required.

### Authentication And Secrets

- Keep human dashboard authentication separate from machine reporter authentication.
- Reuse an existing OIDC identity provider for human login when one is available. Otherwise use a
  maintained MFA-capable Django-compatible mechanism for the single maintainer. Do not stand up a
  new identity-provider service solely for the dashboard.
- Give each reporting instance a revocable, scoped machine credential. A reporter credential cannot
  create a browser session, and a browser session cannot impersonate a reporter.
- Keep production secrets in protected host-local secret files or the platform's secret mechanism,
  never in Git, images, generated bundles, database exports, or reporting payloads.

### Instance Enrollment And Trust

Use an explicit lifecycle: `unregistered` → `pending` → `trusted`, with `rejected`, `expired`, and
`revoked` terminal branches and credential rotation available only from `trusted`.

1. The operator runs the proposed `ts dashboard connect` action. Enrollment must never start merely
   because Tool Shed launches, upgrades, or discovers that no credential exists.
2. The installation creates a stable user-local instance ID, separate from the project ID, and
   sends a sanitized request to `/api/v1/enrollments`. The request may include an operator-facing
   label, project ID, instance ID, Tool Shed/client protocol versions, general platform, and exact
   requested reporting scopes. It sends no paths, usernames, document bodies, source, logs, or
   credentials.
3. The server creates an untrusted pending record and returns a high-entropy device code, a short
   human verification code, `https://ts.rookaro.com/dashboard/` as the verification entry point, a
   short expiration such as ten minutes, and a minimum polling interval.
4. The authenticated dashboard shows a pending-instance notification. Before approval, the operator
   compares the locally displayed code and reviews the project label and ID, instance ID, version,
   platform, and requested scopes. Approval and rejection are explicit, audited actions.
5. The instance polls the enrollment result with the high-entropy device code until approval,
   rejection, or expiration. The server does not call back into the local installation.
6. Approval returns a randomly generated 256-bit opaque reporter token exactly once. The server
   stores only a strong verifier or hash. In the first release, the client stores the token through
   Tool Shed's existing protected user-local credential mechanism outside the repository, project
   SQLite database, Tool Shed snapshot, logs, and generated views. Operating-system-specific
   keyring integrations are deferred.
7. The reporter token is scoped only to heartbeat and sanitized state reporting for its instance.
   It cannot authenticate a human dashboard session, read other projects, mutate local or hosted
   work, or approve another instance.
8. Ordinary requests use HTTPS plus the scoped credential, instance and project IDs, monotonic
   sequence, idempotency key, timestamp, and protocol version. Token authentication and message
   deduplication remain separate controls.
9. The dashboard exposes pending, trusted, expired, rejected, and revoked instances with last seen,
   credential age, client version, audit history, rename, reject, rotate, and immediate revoke
   controls. Rotation uses a bounded overlap window; lost or revoked credentials require pairing
   again.

The same project may have several approved instances, but each installation has its own instance ID
and credential. Copying project files must not copy instance trust. A reinstall or credential-state
loss produces a new pending request rather than silently reclaiming an earlier identity.

Protect the unauthenticated enrollment endpoint with TLS, request-size limits, expiration,
single-use codes, bounded polling, per-source and global rate limits, and a cap on visible pending
requests. Notifications should collect in a dashboard badge or queue rather than create an
unbounded interrupt stream. Approval should require a recent authenticated human session and the
matching short code.

### Deployment And Operations

- Compose services should be limited initially to `gateway`, `dashboard`, `database`, and a
  one-shot `migrate` action. Use a host scheduler or bounded one-shot command for backups and
  retention; do not add Redis, Celery, Kubernetes, or a permanent queue service without measured
  need.
- Expose only nginx on host port `8087`; keep the application and PostgreSQL on an internal network,
  with no published database port.
- Provide separate gateway liveness, application liveness, and database/schema readiness checks.
- Build versioned immutable application and static-site artifacts, record application/API/schema
  versions, run backward-compatible migrations before cutover, and retain the preceding release
  plus a verified database backup for rollback.
- Back up through PostgreSQL-aware tooling rather than copying live database storage, define
  retention, and prove restoration before claiming production readiness.
- Preserve the current public route while validating the new stack; activate `/dashboard/` only
  after internal health, authentication, ingestion, rendering, backup, and rollback gates pass.

### Presentation Qualification

- Capture desktop and mobile reference screenshots for representative public routes before the
  stack migration.
- Reuse the current semantic shell and shared visual tokens. Namespace dashboard-only CSS and
  JavaScript so they cannot silently restyle public documentation.
- Add visual-regression, route, internal-link, accessibility, and asset-cache tests for the existing
  site as deployment gates.
- Treat an unchanged HTTP status as insufficient: compare rendered presentation and interaction at
  representative desktop and mobile widths.

## Instance Reporting Contract

### Local Pipeline

1. A relevant Tool Shed mutation records a sanitized reporting event in a durable local outbox.
2. A lightweight launcher obtains an atomic lock keyed by stable Tool Shed instance identity.
3. If the lock is already held, the launcher exits; the active worker discovers the event on its
   next one-second outbox poll.
4. Otherwise the launcher starts one detached worker for that instance while retaining the lock.
5. The worker polls the outbox every second, drains queued events, acknowledges successful
   delivery, and sends a heartbeat every one minute.
6. Failed delivery uses bounded exponential backoff with jitter and never creates a launch storm.
7. After two idle hours with an empty outbox, the worker sends `quiescent` and exits.
8. A scheduler or equivalent safety mechanism runs a low-cost periodic check to detect missed
   triggers, direct database changes, stale locks, crashes, and pending events.

### Duplicate And Ordering Protection

- Use an atomic operating-system lock; do not trust a PID file alone. A control socket is not part
  of the first release because one-second outbox polling provides the required wakeup bound.
- Recover stale locks and verify process identity before replacing local coordination state.
- Give every update an instance-scoped monotonic sequence and idempotency key.
- Make server ingestion idempotent so retries and out-of-order delivery cannot duplicate or regress
  reported state.
- Keep one worker per Tool Shed instance. A global multi-project daemon is not required initially.

### Server Presence States

- `active`: last heartbeat is within two minutes.
- `delayed`: an active client has missed expected heartbeats for two to five minutes or reports
  queued delivery retries.
- `stale`: no expected heartbeat or safety report arrives after five minutes and the instance did
  not declare quiescence.
- `quiescent`: the worker intentionally stopped after the two-hour inactivity window.
- `incompatible`: the client and server cannot safely exchange their protocol versions.

The exact stale presentation may later account for a declared `next_check_at` safety interval so an
intentionally sleeping installation is not shown as failed.

### Heartbeat Payload

Keep active heartbeats small: stable instance ID, project ID, status, sequence, timestamp, client
version, and protocol version. Do not resend full project state every minute. Send detailed state
only when its export revision changes or when the server requests an authorized resynchronization.

### App Server Health And Failure Payload

- Report one monotonic counter epoch plus cumulative App Server execution-attempt, App Server
  failure, and GUI-fallback counts. A reinstall, explicit counter reset, or incompatible local
  telemetry migration starts a new epoch rather than making counters appear to move backward.
- Report enabled/configured state, local qualification or availability state, client/App Server
  version, general platform, last-success and last-failure time, and a bounded sanitized failure
  category/signature. Send a new snapshot only when these values change or during the periodic
  safety report; do not attach it to every one-minute heartbeat.
- Classify App Server transport, startup, protocol, authentication, compatibility, timeout, and
  unavailable-path failures separately from campaign, tool, test, or product-result failures.
- Deduplicate repeated occurrences locally and server-side while preserving occurrence count,
  first/last observed time, affected version/platform cohort, and whether GUI fallback succeeded.
- Never upload prompts, source, tool arguments, absolute paths, raw stdout/stderr, stack traces,
  credentials, or unrestricted logs. Any retained example must be produced by a versioned local
  sanitizer and bounded by an explicit allowlist.
- Expose a maintainer-authenticated read-only query/export for selected instances, versions,
  periods, or failure groups. The result is diagnostic input; it does not authorize or execute a
  mitigation.

### Remedial Work And Token-Efficiency Payload

- Accept only the deterministic aggregate emitted by the local IDEA-0011 measurement contract. The
  payload identifies project and instance, schema version, measurement window, counter epoch,
  generated time, journal freshness, and provider surface.
- Report exact `remedial_tokens_actual` only as the sum of model-turn token deltas attached to
  locally classified remedial phases whose provider exposed usage. Report the measured-operation
  numerator and eligible-operation denominator as `remedial_token_coverage`; missing token usage is
  unknown and never converted to zero or an estimate.
- Report remedial tool calls, output bytes, duration, retry count, and classified phase counts as
  independent portable proxies. Do not add actual tokens and proxies together or use a weighted
  proxy as if it were an actual-token total.
- Send aggregate snapshots on material change or the periodic safety pass, not every one-minute
  heartbeat. Preserve idempotency and monotonic counter or window semantics across retries.
- Never upload prompts, model responses, command text, tool arguments, source, absolute paths, raw
  stdout/stderr, stack traces, or journal entries. The server stores only allowlisted aggregates
  needed for per-project presentation and bounded trend windows.

## Authority, Privacy, And Reliability Boundaries

- Local SQLite remains authoritative. The hosted dashboard is advisory and read-only in the first
  release; it cannot mutate campaigns, roadmaps, outcomes, or project files.
- Send only allowlisted status fields. Do not upload source, prompts, logs, credentials, local
  absolute paths, full databases, owner-authored document bodies, or raw evidence.
- Treat App Server counters and sanitized failure groups as operational telemetry with explicit
  retention, access, reset/epoch, and deletion rules. The dashboard must identify incomplete or
  stale telemetry instead of presenting missing reports as zero failures.
- Treat remedial-work aggregates as operational measurements, not productivity scores or project
  truth. Always present token coverage, freshness, provider surface, window, and epoch with the
  totals; do not compare partial and complete coverage as equivalent.
- Use stable project and instance identities so several installations and projects do not collide.
- Authenticate outbound HTTPS reports with a revocable instance-specific credential stored outside
  tracked project state.
- Network or dashboard failure must never block ordinary Tool Shed work.
- Bound and compact the local outbox while retaining the newest complete state needed for recovery.
- Include client/server protocol negotiation, rate limits, retry guidance, retention policy, and
  observable last-success/last-error state.
- GitHub and an initialized Git repository are not production runtime requirements.

## Upgrade Contract

- A running worker should finish or safely retain its current delivery, then exit when it detects a
  newer installed reporter version. The next trigger starts the new version.
- Pending outbox records must remain readable across supported upgrades, with explicit migrations
  and rollback evidence for incompatible schema changes.
- Upgrades must not replace a live worker underneath an unacknowledged delivery without recovery.
- The server accepts a bounded compatibility window and reports `incompatible` rather than silently
  misinterpreting an old payload.
- App Server counter epochs and failure-category schema versions must survive supported upgrades;
  incompatible telemetry is reported as unknown/incompatible rather than merged into misleading
  totals.
- Remedial-work aggregate schema, counter epochs, coverage semantics, and reporting windows must
  survive supported upgrades; incompatible or reset series remain visibly separate.
- The maintainer workspace and ordinary installed instances require separate qualification because
  their upgrade paths differ.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Preserve the existing `ts.rookaro.com` public presentation intact as an explicit compatibility
  requirement, while keeping public documentation navigation separate from the authenticated
  dashboard application shell.
- Use `https://ts.rookaro.com/dashboard/` as the canonical dashboard landing page. A versioned
  reporting API uses `/api/v1/` behind the same nginx gateway.
- Stack replacement is permitted only when it provides a concrete functionality, reliability,
  speed, security, deployment, or recovery benefit and preserves qualified public presentation.
- Do not make the hosted server an authority for local work state.
- Do not add remote command execution, campaign control, file editing, or server-to-instance
  mutation in the first release.
- Do not put a mitigation button on the hosted App Server screen. Mitigation begins only after the
  operator enters the Tool Shed maintainer, requests the relevant hosted report, and authorizes the
  resulting local work through normal Tool Shed governance.
- Do not require an always-running process after the two-hour idle window.
- Do not equate intentional quiescence with failure.
- Do not interpret deferred presentation, identity, database, or streaming options as first-release
  requirements without new evidence and an explicit scope decision.
- Do not support both SQLite and PostgreSQL as hosted-server database choices in the first release;
  PostgreSQL is the single hosted store while SQLite remains local Tool Shed authority.
- Do not reuse deferred completion-watcher/email scope as if it were this dashboard's approved
  direction; use that work only as relevant protocol, auth, privacy, and failure-model evidence.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Singleton event-driven worker with two-hour idle timeout | Immediate changes, active heartbeat, bounded resident lifetime | Needs robust locking, wakeup, detachment, and crash recovery | Selected direction |
| Permanent per-instance daemon | Simplest continuous heartbeat semantics | Persistent installation and supervision burden | Not selected initially |
| One-shot report on every mutation | Very simple lifecycle | Repeated startup, weak retry coordination, no active heartbeat | Insufficient alone |
| Scheduled one-shot safety reporter | Recovers missed triggers and sleeping clients | Updates are not immediate by itself | Required backstop |
| Server polls each installation | Central scheduling | Requires inbound reachability and expands local attack surface | Rejected |
| Read-only advisory dashboard | Keeps local authority and limits blast radius | Cannot manage work remotely | Required first-release boundary |
| Remote dashboard control | Could centralize operations | Authentication, authorization, replay, conflict, and safety risk | Explicitly deferred |
| Retain static nginx-only deployment | No application migration and excellent public-doc speed | Cannot provide authenticated dynamic views, ingestion, sessions, or durable multi-instance state | Insufficient for the dashboard |
| Nginx gateway plus Django and PostgreSQL | Keeps public pages fast while adding mature sessions, migrations, API handling, concurrency, and recovery | Adds two managed services and database operations | Selected hosted direction |
| Rewrite the whole site as a client-side SPA | One frontend runtime | High presentation-regression, accessibility, caching, and dependency cost with no public-doc benefit | Rejected |
| Add Redis, Celery, or Kubernetes initially | Supports broader scale and asynchronous orchestration | Operational complexity without demonstrated load | Deferred until measured need |
| Instance-initiated device-style pairing | No inbound workstation route; operator sees and approves exact trust request | Requires expiring codes, bounded polling, notification-abuse controls, and credential lifecycle | Selected enrollment direction |
| Dashboard creates a token for manual copy | Simple server implementation | Copy/paste exposure, unclear instance binding, and avoidable operator handling | Rejected as the normal path |
| Mutual TLS or custom request signing in v1 | Strong key-bound identity | Certificate/signature lifecycle and proxy complexity before the threat model proves need | Deferred; do not invent custom cryptography |
| Bounded browser polling | Simple same-origin refresh with few moving parts | Updates are periodic rather than pushed | Selected for first release; reconsider SSE only with evidence |

## Open Questions

- Which existing OIDC provider should authenticate human dashboard sessions, or which maintained
  MFA-capable fallback should be selected if no provider is available?
- What observed threat, deployment scale, or credential-loss evidence would justify replacing the
  initial opaque reporter token with a standard key-bound or mutual-TLS mechanism?
- Which exact sanitized fields and derived status categories make up reporting API v1?
- Should the safety check default to 15 or 30 minutes, and how should it run across Linux, macOS,
  and Windows without making one scheduler mandatory?
- How should the server group instances that report the same project ID from different machines?
- What retention window is useful for material state-change events?
- What retention window and occurrence cap preserve useful App Server failure groupings without
  turning the dashboard into a raw log collector?
- Which exact local events increment App Server attempt, failure, and fallback counters, and where
  should their durable counter epoch live so upgrades and reinstalls remain unambiguous?
- What maintainer-side command and report format should retrieve a selected server cohort while
  keeping hosted credentials and diagnostic results out of project state and logs?
- What fleet size and heartbeat load must the first deployment support?
- Which existing completion-watcher protocol and failure-model results can be reused without
  importing its deferred email scope?
- What backup cadence, retention window, recovery-time target, and restoration evidence should gate
  the PostgreSQL production cutover?

## Decisions

- Build one centralized, authenticated, cross-project status dashboard with the canonical landing
  page `https://ts.rookaro.com/dashboard/`.
- Preserve the current public site's visual presentation, responsive behavior, routes, and static
  documentation performance as a compatibility contract.
- Permit stack changes when they provide a concrete benefit and pass public-presentation regression
  qualification.
- Keep nginx as the only externally exposed `ts.rookaro.com` service and route static public pages,
  `/dashboard/`, and `/api/v1/` inside the existing hostname boundary.
- Keep first-release dashboard and public-site source in the existing
  `PC-Redemption/tool_shed` GitHub repository. Treat generated builds and the `sup` deployment copy
  as release outputs, and keep live state and secrets outside Git.
- Use one Django application for the authenticated dashboard and reporting API, backed by
  one PostgreSQL instance without first-release HA or replicas. Keep local Tool Shed SQLite state
  authoritative and do not offer server-side SQLite as an alternate deployment mode.
- Limit first-release project detail to Overview, Current Work, Outcome Reconciliation, and Health;
  defer pinned/grouped navigation, advanced filters, detailed roadmap/history views, and SSE.
- Reuse existing OIDC when available; otherwise use maintained Django-compatible MFA for the single
  maintainer rather than introducing a new identity-provider service.
- Keep human session authentication and instance reporter credentials separate and revocable.
- Use instance-initiated, operator-approved, device-style enrollment with expiring short codes and
  outbound client polling; the enrollment request itself grants no trust.
- Issue one scoped 256-bit opaque reporter token after approval, store only its verifier server-side,
  and keep the client token in protected user-local credential storage outside project state.
- Support audited approval, rejection, expiration, rotation, and immediate revocation. Require new
  pairing after reinstall, credential loss, or revocation.
- Do not add Redis, Celery, Kubernetes, a message broker, or a full SPA without measured need.
- Keep each local Tool Shed instance authoritative and make the hosted service advisory/read-only
  for the first release.
- Add a dedicated informational App Server fleet screen with per-instance cumulative use, failure,
  and fallback counts; current health and qualification state; version/platform cohorts; and
  sanitized, deduplicated failure groups.
- Add an informational Work Efficiency screen grouped by project, with exact measured remedial
  tokens plus coverage and separately labelled interaction/output/duration/retry proxies; never
  infer GUI token usage or hide partial measurement.
- Keep App Server telemetry out of one-minute heartbeat history. Report monotonic counter epochs and
  health snapshots only on material change or the periodic safety pass.
- Keep mitigation in the Tool Shed maintainer. The hosted site supplies information only; a
  maintainer-side read-only request retrieves the selected server report before governed local
  diagnosis or mitigation.
- Establish a reusable header, footer, left navigation pane, and right content pane template.
- Use one level of URL-addressable content tabs to mitigate long pages.
- Keep attention state and summary cards visible above the tabs.
- Use one singleton background reporting worker per Tool Shed instance.
- Reuse an active worker by letting a duplicate launcher exit when the atomic lock is held; the
  worker polls the durable outbox every second, so no first-release control socket is required.
- Emit active heartbeats every one minute.
- Exit after two hours without activity only when the durable outbox is empty, after declaring
  quiescence.
- Add a periodic safety check so direct database writes, missed triggers, and crashes converge.
- Use durable outbox delivery, idempotency keys, monotonic sequence numbers, bounded retry with
  jitter, and server-side deduplication.
- Update current presence and `last_seen` in place and retain material state changes only; do not
  build heartbeat-history or rollup machinery in the first release.
- Open the PRM outcome loop from `IDEA-0007` and require origin-to-product reconciliation; child
  lifecycle completion does not by itself close the idea.

## Don't Forget

- One-minute heartbeats should be tiny presence messages, not full-state uploads.
- Missing or stale App Server telemetry is unknown, not a zero-use or zero-failure result.
- Count App Server-path failures separately from failures in work executed successfully through App
  Server; otherwise the health screen will punish healthy transport for product/test failures.
- A successful GUI fallback preserves work continuity but remains visible because it may conceal an
  App Server reliability regression.
- Failure collection must aggregate sanitized signatures and counts, not upload raw logs for later
  cleanup.
- The server should update current presence in place rather than retain heartbeat history.
- A successful HTTP response proves ingestion, not that the dashboard reflects correct local
  outcome truth; reconciliation and projection checks still matter.
- Duplicate-process prevention and duplicate-message prevention are separate requirements.
- Direct SQLite access should not corrupt reporting. The next managed trigger or scheduled safety
  pass must detect and report the resulting state change.
- The operator's primary workflow is rapidly switching among many concurrently active projects.
- Dashboard alerts must prioritize actionable state instead of turning every sleeping project into
  noise.
- Public presentation preservation needs visual evidence, not only successful status codes or unit
  tests.
- The static public site, authenticated dashboard, reporting API, and PostgreSQL data each need an
  independently observable health and rollback boundary.
- Database migration rollback and application rollback are not equivalent; migrations must remain
  backward-compatible through the cutover window.
- A pending enrollment record is untrusted data and must never appear as an active reporting
  instance until the authenticated operator approves the matching code.
- Project identity and instance identity are different: one project may have multiple independently
  trusted installations, and repository copies must not carry machine credentials.
- Enrollment notification spam is part of the security model; expiry, rate limits, pending caps,
  and an in-dashboard queue are required from the first release.

